{% extends "_base.html.tera" %}

{% block title %}Oracle · EscrowNad{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<main class="app-main app-main--prose">
  <section>
    <h1 class="app-main__title">What is the oracle?</h1>
    <p class="lead">EscrowNad settles <strong>USDC</strong> on external proof — not “trust the seller”.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Flow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Seller</strong> lists an IPv4 block (PA or PI) and the intended transfer (from → to).</li>
      <li><strong>Buyer</strong> deposits <strong>USDC</strong> into the on-chain lock on <strong>Monad</strong> (after ERC-20 approve).</li>
      <li><strong>Observer</strong> (Rust) polls public RIPE transfer tables.</li>
      <li>Matching row → <strong>release USDC</strong> to seller.</li>
      <li>Deadline with no fact → <strong>refund USDC</strong> to buyer.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>RIPE sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>PI (assignments): <code>transfers-assignments.json</code></li>
      <li>PA (allocations): <code>transfers-allocations.json</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      Match key: resource kind + prefix/block + parties.
      Classic IPv4 brokers use human escrow; we automate release on public fact.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/deals/">Browse deals →</a></p>
  </section>
</main>
{% endblock %}
